{
  "abstract": "Paper 83 deletes a cut $D$ from a random set $S$ (density $2/n$) so that no line of primitive height at least $\\theta n$ holds three points. It then refills free cells to recover $|S|$ points, with a martingale argument, whose stopping-time step is sketched, for $\\theta \\ge 0.27$. We give three further sufficient criteria for refill to succeed, each certified in exact rational arithmetic (with interval bounds for exponentials) from the clipped-rectangle geometry of Paper 83.\n- (i) Random-order refill, which has the law of uniform refill, succeeds for $\\theta \\ge 0.215$.\n- (ii) For $\\theta \\ge 1/4$ refill succeeds in every order, by a deterministic count.\n- (iii) A potential that charges only influence still present on free cells gives a criterion in terms of the realized $S$ and $D$. It holds at $\\theta = 1/4$ with margin $0.1007$ and at $\\theta = 6/25$, against $0.0010$ for the retained-influence criterion it sharpens.\n\nOn Model A, (iii) does not extend the range given by (i). Its use is for starting sets other than the product law. These fixed-band results give no all-slope density improvement; lower heights and axis quotas remain uncontrolled.",
